MANIFEST

# Security - Never commit secrets
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
*.key
*.pem
*private*

# Sealed credentials: CIPHERTEXT IS TRACKED, everything else is not.
#
# This mirrors the fix already made in fleet-ops. hee-cred's own docstring
# says storage is "git-tracked, ciphertext only", and hee-pve-health reads
# a sealed token from <repo>/.hee/secrets/. A bare `secrets/` rule made
# both false HERE, in the repo that ships those tools: the directory could
# never be committed, so sealed material lived on exactly one machine and
# died with it. That is recorded as a known_blocker in
# contracts/fleet-identity-v1.contract.yaml and is disqualifying for
# identity key material.
#
# Measured 2026-09-03: `hee view --nerd` reported "pve: unreachable" for a
# second operator on the same LAN. It was not unreachable -- the sealed
# token path had never worked for anyone, and the only thing that did work
# was an untracked per-user ~/.config/fleet-ops/pve-api.env.
#
# Exclude the CONTENTS, not the directory -- git cannot re-include a file
# whose parent directory is excluded, so `secrets/` would make the
# negation below inert.
#
# The negations that used to sit here (`!secrets/*.gpg`, `!.hee/secrets/*.gpg`)
# were copied from fleet-ops, where they are correct: that repo is PRIVATE and
# really does track 7 sealed files. This repo is PUBLIC and tracks zero. The
# exception therefore had no users here and only one effect -- it let `git add`
# stage a sealed credential without complaint, which is exactly what happened
# in pr:609@human-execution-engine, inside a documentation typo PR.
#
# Sealed is not safe once the ciphertext is public and permanent: it moves the
# problem from "nobody can reach it" to "nobody can break the cipher for as
# long as this repo exists", and undoing it costs a history rewrite plus a
# rotation anyway. So: no exception here. Sealed secrets live in a private
# repo, or outside git.
secrets/*
.hee/secrets/*

