# GC Report Pack outputs (generated)
/var/gc/

# HEE local state. Real fix, 2026-08-21: a bare `.hee` exclusion here,
# with !.hee/spool/**-style negations meant to re-include specific
# subdirectories, never actually worked -- git's own documented
# limitation ("It is not possible to re-include a file if a parent
# directory of that file is excluded") means the negations were dead
# code; .hee/spool/ and .hee/evidence/ are only tracked today because
# their files were force-added once, before anyone hit this. Correct
# fix: don't exclude .hee/ itself at all -- only the specific
# subdirectory that's genuinely local-only. spool/, evidence/,
# tickets/ are untracked-by-default like anything else not listed here.
.hee/notes/

# Local cache of vendored governance docs -- this repo IS their source, so
# the copy under .hee/ is a build artifact of the vendor sync, not content.
#
# Added 2026-09-02 when the machine-local `.hee/` blanket came out of
# .git/info/exclude. That blanket silently re-imposed exactly what the
# 2026-08-21 fix above removed, in every clone, invisibly -- the rule was
# not in any tracked file, so nobody reading the repo could see it.
#
# Comment on its own line, deliberately: git does NOT support a trailing
# comment on a pattern, so `.hee/policy/  # cache` is a literal pattern
# that matches nothing. Written that way first in fleet-ops the same day
# and it silently staged 110 files.
.hee/policy/

# node
node_modules/

# local build outputs
/cmd/hee/hee
/cmd/hee/cmd/

# committed evidence (track)
!hee/
!hee/evidence/
!hee/**

# hee: generated ui output (do not commit)
generated/html/
.venv-claude/

# hee: env for pve api comms
hee/docs/00-hee-deploy-env-setup.sh
.claude/worktrees/

# mandb writes its whatis/apropos index into every directory on the manpath.
# `make manpath` in dotfiles registers man/ and man/tools/, so running it (or
# any `mandb`) drops binary index.db files into this checkout. Derived state,
# never committed -- and without this rule a single `git add -A` commits binary
# blobs, which is exactly how a 53 KB coverage binary landed here before.
man/**/index.db
man/index.db
